\paragraph{Simulation dataset}
Current external-data simulations use the oceanography-waves-lib v1.3.0
28 ft (8.53 m) fin-keel sailboat RAO records at 5 kn. Heading oscillates
$\pm3^\circ$ about north with a 120 s sine period. The incident wave field
is sampled along the moving track; the estimated response uses instantaneous
heading and signed encounter frequency. Records include turning acceleration,
response modulation, rotating-frame terms and full yaw--pitch--roll body rates.
A stationary sea condition denotes a steady incident spectrum; the vessel
continues to move. Raw displacement and velocity include total CG travel. Wave-estimator
comparisons express reference displacement and velocity relative to the
known cruise track, while retaining all recorded specific force and total
acceleration. Body axes are $x$ forward, $y$ port and $z$ up.

The RAOs are analytical estimates, not measured hull-specific responses.
The underway model is quasi-steady; it does not identify hull-specific
forward-speed hydrodynamics or propulsion/rudder dynamics. Filename heights,
periods and supplied spectra identify the incident sea, not the encounter
period or vessel-motion spectrum. JONSWAP and PM records use first-order
incident components; regular-wave elevation harmonics pass through the
response. Largest cases remain extrapolated stress tests. The default IMU
is at the centre of gravity; lever-arm studies add an explicit sensor offset.
Sections explicitly identified as archived v1.2.3 comparisons retain their
original zero-speed experiment and are not underway evidence.
